  "query_preview": "..\n\nA split structure to the baroclinic zone exists across the Great \nLakes region with the surface dewpoint discontinuity/front well to \nthe south of the midlevel frontal extension. Upper level jet streak \nwill progress through the basal portion of the composite trough axis \nthis afternoon bringing an increase in anticyclonic flow \ntrajectories. So while Southeast Michigan will become increasingly \non the cold side of the boundaries, the greatest dcva with the \nabsolute vorticity maximum will occur in the 18-00z time window. \nModels continue to support modest 870-700mb fgen response 21-01z \nassociated with the vorticity maximum primarily north of I 69. \nThought about increasing PoPs into the likely range, but with lean \n1000-700mb mean RH decided to stay with PoPs of around 30%.\n\nExtensive stable surface high pressure will build directly through \nSoutheast Michigan tonight and Monday. 850mb temperatures dropping \n+3C to +5C and a light wind environment will result in chilly 40s \nfor Monday morning lows. There could be a few locations, Midland \nCounty, that could see upper 30s but given peak evapotranspiration \nof vegetation believe that temperatures will remain buoyed to some \nextent. Highs Monday afternoon, near 70, are expected to be some 3 \nto 5 degrees below normal.\n\nDynamic upper level low pressure is progged to eject out of the \nRockies through the Northern Plains and into far western Ontario \nMonday night. Taste of Autumn with the strength of the pressure \ngradient bringing strong southerly flow and associated moisture \ntransport to Southeast Michigan Tuesday. Current forecast suggests a \nnoteworthy surge in surface dewpoints above 70 degrees with south \nwinds of 15 to 30 mph. Questions remain on how progressive the band \nof precipitation will be or whether it will remain intact as there \nare some signals that upper level ridging could hold across Metro \nDetroit until late Tuesday. Low level jet becomes veered with \ntrajectories due westerly so southern portions of the forecast area \ncould very well be split by the best forcing. Ensemble guidance \nsuggests its may take until Wednesday to get more meaningful \nprecipitation chances into the area as shortwave energy continues to \nring around the upper level ridging to the south. Many moving parts \nhere but the interquartile range of 24hr QPF does increase to .25-\n0.5 inch by the first half of the day Wednesday for Metro Detroit. \nSecondary increase in synoptic scale forcing appears set for late \nWednesday and Tuesday as another upper level jetlet and right \nentrance region dynamics stripe across the area. Increase in \nageostrophic circulation could result in stalling warm front and \npersistent upward vertical motion. From this vantage point the most \nlikely timeframe for heavy rainfall potential may be Thursday \nmorning.",
